If you are an AI agent and this folder was just opened, read this file top to
bottom first. It is the single source of truth for this folder — it works for
any agent (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, Cursor, and ones not listed here).

## What This Is

This folder is {{#if user_name}}{{user_name}}'s{{else}}your{{/if}} local AIOS: durable, local-first context that
helps AI agents understand who the user is, their active work, priorities, and
communication preferences. It is plain text the user owns. No servers, no cloud.

{{#if vault_path}}
Long-term knowledge is configured at `{{vault_path}}`.
{{else}}
Long-term knowledge is configured at `vault/`.
{{/if}}

## Where Things Live

| Folder | What |
|---|---|
| `context/` | Who the user is, what they are working on, current priorities |
| `projects/` | Active work, one folder per project |
| `memory/` | Recent operational memory; load only the routed slices |
| `vault/` | Long-term knowledge, loaded on demand |
| `connections/` | Service registry and API notes |
| `skills/` | Reusable markdown workflows |
| `plugins/` | Optional extensions with declared permissions |
| `decisions/log.md` | Append-only architectural and product decisions |

## Read Order

1. `context/identity.md`
2. `context/priorities.md`
3. `context/work.md`
4. `context/north-star.md`
5. If working on a project: `projects/<slug>/README.md`
6. Load declared domain files from `context/domains/<domain>.md`

## Memory Routing

- `memory/events.jsonl`: load the last 50 entries only.
- `memory/signals/`: load today and yesterday only.
- `memory/errors.jsonl`: load only when debugging failed operations.
- `vault/`: load on demand when the task references knowledge, companies, people, writing style, or raw sources.
- `memory/sessions/`: at the start of a work session, load the single most recent file from `memory/sessions/` if one exists. This gives you continuity from the last session without loading the full history.

## Project Convention

Each project should have `projects/<slug>/README.md` with frontmatter:

```yaml
---
project: example-project
domain: [build]
status: active
---
```

Available domains:

- `make`: creative output, content, publishing
- `sell`: outreach, applications, positioning, proposals
- `build`: software, systems, automations

## Skills

Skills are reusable workflows in `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` — plain markdown
instruction sets. **Any agent can run one**: open that skill's `SKILL.md` and
follow it. Run a skill when the user invokes it by name or asks for that workflow.

`skills/INDEX.md` is the live list of every skill installed in this folder, each
with a one-line description. It is auto-generated and kept current — read it to
see what is available.

`skills/RESOLVER.md` is the routing table: it maps what the user might say
(trigger phrases) to the skill that handles it. To answer a request, match the
user's intent against RESOLVER.md, then open that skill's `SKILL.md` and follow
it. Prefer the most specific match. Both files are auto-generated.

For a programmatic match, run `dotaios skills resolve "<intent>"` (or call the
MCP `resolve_skill` tool). It returns the ranked skills that fit the intent, with
the `SKILL.md` path to open. Exit code 2 means nothing fits, hand-roll the work
and offer to skillify a repeat. `dotaios skills resolve --boot-context` prints a
Markdown `## Skills first` prompt block for fleet scripts. Capture it as text
with `BOOT_CONTEXT="$(dotaios skills resolve --boot-context)"`, then append that
variable to the agent prompt.

### Installing new skills

If the user gives you a repository URL and asks you to install a skill or plugin:
1. Clone the repository to a temporary folder: `git clone <url> /tmp/dotaios-plugin`
2. Install it locally: `npx dotaios install /tmp/dotaios-plugin`
3. Clean up: `rm -rf /tmp/dotaios-plugin`
4. Confirm to the user that it is installed and summarize what the new skill does.

## Rules

- Treat `context/` as always relevant.
- Treat `memory/` as recent operational state, not an infinite transcript.
- Treat `vault/` as routed long-term knowledge.
- When the user shares a URL, run `dotaios ingest <url>` to read and save it. This uses a local headless browser that renders JavaScript. Prefer it over your own web fetch tools.
- Never expose secrets.
- Ask before writing durable identity, CRM, or wiki knowledge.
- Suggest a new skill when a workflow repeats: offer to run the `skillify` skill, which drafts a new skill (with trigger phrases) and asks for approval before saving it. Never save a generated skill without the user's OK.
- At the start of a work session, if `memory/inbox/` contains files, read them before anything else and use the `process-inbox` skill to file each one into the right place, then delete the inbox file. These are notes captured on another device.
- At the start of a work session and again before finishing, run `dotaios sync tick` to keep this folder mirrored to its GitHub repo across devices. It does nothing if cross-device sync is not set up.
- When a work session is wrapping up, offer to save it: "Want me to save this session to your AIOS memory?" Use the `save-session` skill.
